body:
An MCP App's `csp` block carries four domain lists that widen the policy its frame runs under. The MCP Apps schema documents each of them as origins:

```ts
export interface McpUiResourceCsp {
    /** @description Origins for network requests (fetch/XHR/WebSocket). */
    connectDomains?: string[];
    /** @description Origins for static resources (scripts, images, styles, fonts). */
    resourceDomains?: string[];
    /** @description Origins for nested iframes (frame-src directive). */
    frameDomains?: string[];
    /** @description Allowed base URIs for the document (base-uri directive). */
    baseUriDomains?: string[];
}
```

`_injectPreamble` passed the values through with HTML special characters escaped and nothing else, so an entry naming a privileged application scheme — `vscode-managed-remote-resource:`, `vscode-file:`, `file:` — reached the emitted policy and widened the frame's reach beyond network origins.

This keeps the entries that are network origins. It enforces what the schema already says these fields hold rather than adding a rule of its own, and the existing escaping is untouched and still does its job. I read the note above `cleanDomains` as being about malformed domain *syntax*, which is a different question from whether a value is an origin at all, so I have left that reasoning and its comment alone.

An offending entry is dropped on its own rather than the list being refused, so one bad value does not cost an app the rest of its policy.

One detail worth a reviewer's eye: a port is not mistaken for a scheme. A host name is a valid scheme spelling, so `example.com:443` has to be read as a host and a port rather than as a scheme source. A scheme source is either `scheme:` on its own or followed by `//`, and anything else is treated as a host source and left alone.

## Testing

`src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat/test/browser/mcpAppCspOrigins.test.ts` adds 9 tests: host sources with and without ports and wildcards, the port-versus-scheme case, the network schemes including `data:` and `blob:` and a mixed-case spelling, the privileged schemes that are dropped, a mixed list keeping its good entries, empty and whitespace entries, and `undefined` passing through.

Unlike a fix to existing logic, these pin new behaviour rather than failing before and passing after — the function is new, so there is nothing for them to fail against. The integration is the single `cleanDomains` call, which now composes the filter ahead of the escaping.

4431 passing across the `chat` and `mcp` suites, against 4405 on an unmodified checkout, with the same 13 failures in both runs. `tsc -p src` and `eslint` are clean.

## Note on reporting

This was reported through MSRC (case 127093) as part of a larger report about the managed remote resource protocol, and closed as defence in depth. The main part of that report — a downstream `file:` scheme reaching `ElectronRemoteResourceLoader` — is already fixed on `main`, which hardcodes the scheme now. This is the remaining piece. I am sending it here rather than opening a public issue describing it, since SECURITY.md asks that issues not be used for this. Happy to open one if you would prefer the PR to reference it.

## Summary
Currently, `GeminiNativeBYOKLMProvider` strictly filters out all models returned by the Google GenAI SDK that are not pre-registered in `this._knownModels`. When Google releases a new model or preview checkpoint (e.g., `gemini-2.5-*`, `gemini-3.7-flash`), users cannot select or use the model until Microsoft updates the remote `copilotChat.json` CDN manifest or ships a patch release.

We propose:
1. Adding a default capabilities fallback in `geminiNativeProvider.ts` (matching the pattern already used in `anthropicProvider.ts`).
2. Adding an optional setting in `settings.json` to allow user-defined Gemini model IDs and capability overrides.

---

## Current Behavior & Code Analysis

In `extensions/copilot/src/extension/byok/vscode-node/geminiNativeProvider.ts`, `getAllModels()` queries Google's API via `client.models.list()`, but discards any model not found in `_knownModels`:

```typescript
// extensions/copilot/src/extension/byok/vscode-node/geminiNativeProvider.ts
const client = new GoogleGenAI({ apiKey });
const models = await client.models.list();
const modelList: Record<string, BYOKModelCapabilities> = {};

for await (const model of models) {
    const modelId = model.name;
    if (!modelId) {
        continue;
    }

    // Strict filter: drops all new / unlisted models
    if (this._knownModels && this._knownModels[modelId]) {
        modelList[modelId] = this._knownModels[modelId];
    }
}
return byokKnownModelsToAPIInfoWithEffort(this._name, modelList);
```

### Inconsistency with Other Providers
In `extensions/copilot/src/extension/byok/vscode-node/anthropicProvider.ts`, unlisted models returned by the API are not discarded; they automatically fall back to baseline capabilities:

```typescript
// extensions/copilot/src/extension/byok/vscode-node/anthropicProvider.ts
for (const model of response.data) {
    if (this._knownModels && this._knownModels[model.id]) {
        modelList[model.id] = this._knownModels[model.id];
    } else {
        // Mix in generic capabilities for models we don't know
        modelList[model.id] = {
            maxInputTokens: 100000,
            maxOutputTokens: 16000,
            name: model.display_name,
            toolCalling: true,
            vision: false,
            thinking: false
        };
    }
}
```

---

## Proposed Solution

### 1. Capability Fallback for Discovered Gemini Models
Update `geminiNativeProvider.ts` to assign standard defaults to models returned by `client.models.list()` when they are missing from `_knownModels`:

```typescript
for await (const model of models) {
    const modelId = model.name;
    if (!modelId) {
        continue;
    }

    if (this._knownModels && this._knownModels[modelId]) {
        modelList[modelId] = this._knownModels[modelId];
    } else {
        // Fallback for newly released or uncatalogued models
        modelList[modelId] = {
            name: model.displayName || modelId,
            maxInputTokens: 128000,
            maxOutputTokens: 8192,
            toolCalling: true,
            vision: true,
            supportsReasoningEffort: modelId.includes('thinking') || modelId.includes('3') ? ['low', 'medium', 'high'] : undefined,
        };
    }
}
```

### 2. User-Defined Custom Model Configuration (`settings.json`)
Allow users to manually register custom/fine-tuned/preview model IDs without waiting for API discovery or CDN manifest updates:

```json
{
  "github.copilot.chat.byok.gemini.customModels": [
    {
      "id": "models/gemini-3.7-flash",
      "name": "Gemini 3.7 Flash",
      "maxInputTokens": 1000000,
      "maxOutputTokens": 65536,
      "toolCalling": true,
      "vision": true
    }
  ]
}
```

---

## Benefits
- **Zero-Day Availability:** Developers can use newly announced Gemini model IDs immediately upon release without waiting for extension or CDN updates.
- **Parity with Existing Providers:** Aligns the Gemini BYOK provider behavior with `AnthropicLMProvider` and `CustomOAIBYOKModelProvider`.
- **Custom / Fine-Tuned Model Support:** Enables Vertex AI / Google AI Studio custom-tuned endpoints and preview versions that may not appear in default manifest lists.

## Description

Restoring a checkpoint while the agent has an open question leaves the question carousel stranded above the input box.

The carousel (and plan review) are docked into `ChatInputPart` by `ChatListItemRenderer`, and are only cleared when the owning response renders again. A checkpoint restore never removes the request — `requestDisablement` → `ChatModel._setDisabledRequests` marks it `shouldBeRemovedOnSend` and fires `setHidden`, and `ChatViewModel.getItems()` filters it out of the transcript. The response therefore never re-renders, and nothing clears what it docked.

### Changes

- **`chatWidget.ts`**: on the model's `setHidden` event, clear the input-part widgets belonging to requests that dropped out of the transcript. Logic sits in a small exported helper, matching the file's existing convention of unit-testable pure helpers.

  ```ts
  export function clearInputWidgetsForHiddenRequests(input: ..., requests: readonly IChatRequestModel[]): void {
      for (const request of requests) {
          if (request.shouldBeRemovedOnSend && !request.shouldBeRemovedOnSend.afterUndoStop) {
              input?.clearQuestionCarousel(request.id);
              input?.clearPlanReview(request.id);
          }
      }
  }
  ```

- The predicate mirrors the `getItems()` filter exactly: a disablement carrying an `afterUndoStop` only hides part of a response, so the request keeps rendering and its widgets are left alone. Redo re-adds the request and the renderer re-docks the widget.

- Uses `inputPartDisposable.value` rather than `this.input`: the renderer skips docking while a request is being edited inline, so widgets always live on the main input part.

- **`chatWidget.test.ts`**: covers kept / partially-undone (`afterUndoStop`) / fully-undone requests.

### Notes for reviewers

The pending question isn't stranded by the removal — the restore actions call `cancelCurrentRequestForSession` first, and `ChatResponseModel.cancel()` dismisses the carousel data. Disposing the part only persists the draft answer.

This also covers the Agents window in the issue screenshot, which shares `ChatWidget` and feeds the same path via `AgentHostSnapshotController.requestDisablement`.

